---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import dream4_1 from '../../assets/images/dream4_1.png';
import alice03final from '../../assets/images/alice-03-final.png';
---
<PageLayout title="alice — Alex Sheen">
  <div class="base_padding">
    <div class="w3-row base_container_padding">
      <div class="w3-half base_container_padding">
        <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
          <div class="projtitle white_text" style="width:100%">
            alice-01
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              <br />
              <div class="vl">
                Blender, AliceVision Meshroom<br />
                2021<br />
              </div>
              <br />
              I often find myself going down YouTube rabbit holes—especially about digital art—and that's how I first discovered <span class="beige_text">photogrammetry</span>. alice-01 was the result. I began by 3D scanning my head and shoulders, then I duplicated the resulting mesh and applied a combination of modifiers to generate the web-like projections.
              <br /><br />
              As for the name, I had just watched Alice in Wonderland and was experimenting with AliceVision Meshroom for the first time—thus the "alice-{'{n}'}" series was born.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="w3-half base_container_padding">
        <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
          <Image src={dream4_1} style="width:100%" alt="alice-01" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="w3-row base_container_padding">
      <div class="w3-half base_container_padding">
        <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
          <div class="projtitle white_text" style="width:100%">
            alice-02
            <div class="projtext" style="width:100%">
              <br />
              <div class="vl">
                Blender, AliceVision Meshroom<br />
                2021<br />
              </div>
              <br />
              alice-02 was inspired by <span class="beige_text">Midge Sinnaeve</span>, a digital artist whose YouTube series explores the techniques behind his procedural and generative works.
              <br /><br />
              For this piece, I photoscanned vines growing on the Kent Chemical Laboratory building at the University of Chicago. I then took a 3D base model of a human bust and unwrapped its mesh to a 2D plane so that I could use a Surface Deform modifier to project the scanned vine geometry onto the unwrapped mesh, allowing the organic texture to conform to the contours of the original form.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="w3-half base_container_padding">
        <div class="proj" style="width:100%">
          <Image src={alice03final} style="width:100%" alt="alice-02" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
